 "answer": "**Probably yes, if the haze readings I found are current.** I can't confirm today's date, so check the live numbers before you go out.\n\n**What I found:** Several live-tracker pages show unhealthy haze in Singapore.\n- One page, with an NEA reading from 15 Oct, 1:00 pm, showed the 24-hour PSI in the unhealthy to very unhealthy range. The highest was 210 in the West.\n- Another, updated 5:00 pm on 15 Oct 2026, showed an official 24-hour PSI of 188 (Unhealthy). It showed 1-hour PM2.5 of about 155 µg/m³ in the West.\n- A third showed a Central PSI of 219 (Very unhealthy), but its data may be a couple of days old.\n\nThese are third-party sites that repeat NEA data, and they don't fully agree with each other. Treat them as a rough picture, not an official reading.\n\n**What to do, by 24-hour PSI band:**\n- **Up to 100 (Good/Moderate):** You don't need a mask.\n- **101–200 (Unhealthy):** Cut down on prolonged or strenuous outdoor activity. Wear a mask if you'll be outside for a while, and especially if you're elderly, pregnant, a child, or have asthma, heart or lung conditions.\n- **201–300 (Very unhealthy):** Stay indoors as much as you can. If you have to go out, wear a mask.\n\n**Which mask:** A surgical or cloth mask won't filter haze particles well. Use an **N95 or equivalent (FFP2/KN95)** that fits snugly on your face.\n\n**To confirm:** Check the NEA website, the myENV app or haze.gov.sg for the current 24-hour PSI and 1-hour PM2.5 in your region. If you tell me roughly where you'll be and whether you have any health conditions, I can give more specific advice.",
